      "Voluntary e-Way Bill closure is not yet available. The proposed feature, which would have let any authorized party close an e-Way Bill immediately after confirmed delivery, is on hold under GSTN Advisory 668. Until it is re-notified, e-Way Bills close automatically on expiry. LoadSaathi still records proof-of-delivery data on every shipment, so transporters will be ready to use voluntary closure the moment it goes live.",
      "LoadSaathi automates e-Way Bill compliance for transporters. When a shipper posts a load on LoadSaathi, the platform automatically links the e-Way Bill Part-A with the assigned transporter's valid TRANS ID for Part-B compliance. This ensures every truck on the platform carries a valid, verifiable e-Way Bill before departure.",
      "Penalties for transporter non-compliance in 2026 can reach up to200% of the tax amount for invoice-to-cargo mismatches. Detention notices are issued in real-time when the analytics engine detects discrepancies between the e-Way Bill and actual cargo movement. Transporters using verified platforms like LoadSaathi avoid these penalties through automated compliance checks.",
      "For truckers and fleet operators, the current position on the 2026 e-Way Bill proposals means one thing: standard compliance remains mandatory, while the two headline changes are on hold. The combination of real-time analytics, GSTN enforcement, and existing documentation rules means every freight movement must still be properly documented from origin to delivery.",
      "Step-by-Step Guide for Transporters: How to Stay Compliant in 2026. Before accepting any load, verify that the shipper has generated a valid e-Way Bill with correct Part-A details. Confirm the cargo description, value, and destination match the e-Way Bill. If any details are incorrect, request the shipper to amend the e-Way Bill before departure.",
      "At pickup, populate Part-B of the e-Way Bill with your vehicle number and TRANS ID before leaving the shipper's premises. Ensure the vehicle number matches the one registered with the transporter. Any mismatch between the e-Way Bill Part-B and the actual vehicle will trigger a flag in the GSTN analytics engine.",
      "During transit, carry the e-Way Bill at all times. At weighbridges and GST checkpoints, present the e-Way Bill along with the GST invoice and transport receipt. If the e-Way Bill has been generated for a different route or vehicle, do not attempt to use it — penalties for e-Way Bill misuse can reach ₹10,000 per incident.",
      "Upon delivery, confirm delivery with the shipper and retain proof of delivery — photographs of delivered goods, signed delivery receipts, or GPS confirmation. Voluntary e-Way Bill closure is on hold under GSTN Advisory 668, so e-Way Bills will continue to close automatically on expiry for now. If and when voluntary closure is re-notified, closing the e-Way Bill within 24 hours of delivery will prevent unauthorized reuse and protect both shipper and transporter from audit discrepancies.",
